Change(genesis='e30427e699992327267add669c3bc989f6d16525ba6f493121eec7778638e0ce', deps=('298f9b20b5a1ffdd34fb41108f7f4c067ad7a6cb11a89687f3cd5e270c003f09',), event=Authored(todo='coldsyncslow', at=datetime(2026, 11, 26, 18, 18, 55, 436916), actor='bmabsout', kind='suzateryTodo', created=datetime(2026, 11, 5, 0, 0, 0), body="A new device's first sync of the conversations runs at the tailnet's speed and the Inbox counts what the badge counts before it ends.", spec=Flat(value=0.35), rationale=("A new device's first sync was measured in hours, and the Inbox and its badge disagreed until it ended.",), category='', waiting_on='lazysync', detail="State on 2026-11-20: a cold device draws its first page in about a second and the Inbox counts what the badge counts. What is left is the whole first sync, which still runs at the pace of the keeper's work for each piece; lazy sync by hash is the planned remedy.\n\nCorrection on 2026-11-20, measured on a deployment from a cold browser profile: the page's frame draws in about a second, but the conversation's words appear only once the whole skeleton has synced, about 25 to 35 seconds on a quiet keeper and about a minute in the first minutes after a restart, while the keeper's compactor runs. A warm load shows the words in 3 to 4 seconds. A tab has read a conversation only whole since 2026-11-18, so the newest conversation waits for nearly all of the sync.\n\nState on 2026-11-24: the first load is measured by a bench and its two waits are removed (releases 2.1.242 and 2.1.243): the inbox is pulled beside the conversations and once more after them, and the first screen's rendered blocks are asked at the link's width. On the generated big store the first round is drawn at about 6 s where it was 13 s over a phone's network, and at 2.4 s where it was 5 s at the deployment's speed. What is left is in docs/COLDSYNC-PLAN-2026-11-24.md: the list by titles, search that says its coverage, and two further stages named only so they are not rebuilt by accident.\n\nState on 2026-11-24: seen after release 2.1.245 made the conversations a page of their own: on a device that holds nothing, that page stays a skeleton of rows until the whole first sync has ended, because the list reads every content object. In work: the plan's first two stages (the list read by titles; search that says what it covers).\n\n2026-11-25, release 2.1.253, deployed: the conversations' list is read by titles. On a device that holds nothing it stands on the skeleton and fills each row in place as its few lines land, each row saying what it still lacks; a search over part of the content says how many conversations it read of how many and reads more as content lands. On the generated large store over a phone's network the list is drawn in about five seconds where it took about fifty. Left for a later stage: turns that say no words other than the box's notes still cost a fetch each on the walk to a row's last words, which needs the skeleton to tell them apart.\n\n2.1.272: the walk to a row's last words passes the turns that say no words (a verb's answer, a model turn with calls, an act), told apart by the step the skeleton already carries, with no byte added per node. The saving falls where a conversation ends on such turns; the generated store has none, so its bench is unchanged.", source=None, subtodos=(), notes=(), note=''))